From: ioswitch <noreply@ioswitch.local>
Subject: [PATCH] Render a memoryless color beside linear ones through a linear backing

Core Animation's passes into an IOSurface (a linear image over the surface's pages, once the
driver renders linear images: nvk-linear-color-attachments.patch) all hold a memoryless second
color, an optimal image. A render pass instance renders a linear color in place only where every
color of it is linear: VK_NV_linear_color_attachment's rule, and NVK's own (nvk_rendering_linear),
which otherwise renders the linear one through its tiled shadow, copied in from the surface over the
render area as the pass loads it and back as it stores it, every pass. Before NVK rendered linear
images at all, the surface was a shadow of the runtime's (shadow.rs): a copy pass before each pass
that renders it and a merge-back dispatch after (the host simulation: 2 NVK passes and a dispatch
per CA pass; on GM20B 15-19 render passes per CA command buffer for 4.2 Metal passes).

Metal neither loads nor stores a memoryless texture, so at a pass's start, where the pass has a
linear color, its other colors are memoryless and it has no depth or stencil attachment, each
memoryless color now renders through a linear backing its texture keeps for the queue (a 2D
texture over a Private buffer of the runtime's, rows 128-byte aligned, Mesa's NVK linear layout;
kept_linear_backing), copied nothing, and every color of the pass is linear. Where the device would
not make that texture a linear image, the pass renders as before.

And with unified memory, a pass that clears a bound linear attachment (VK_NV_linear_color_attachment
devices) clears it in place: the choice to clear an image of the device's and copy it back was
measured over host memory across a bus (an RTX 3090 and 5080).
